  "known_gaps": {
    "summary": "The main gaps concern mechanism identification, independent replication, contributor shares, matched ablation comparisons, measurement scope, attribution causality, and evidence verification.",
    "items": [
      "Small effects of ablation and overcorrection do not uniquely identify active compensation. Redundant pathways, or opposing responses in later layers that are proportional to the change, remain alternative explanations.",
      "Independent replication of head-level self-repair on general text remains limited. Existing GPT-2 Small evidence from studies with overlapping authors does not establish broad replication, while variation across prompts and overcorrection still need further testing.",
      "The combined share of repair due to backup heads that take over, LayerNorm rescaling, and neurons that reduce suppression (anti-erasure neurons) remains untested. Identifying contributors does not establish how much total recovery they explain.",
      "No independent group has compared the fraction of output restored under matched zero, mean, and resample ablations. Whole-layer mean ablation remains untested, and whether particular mean replacements preserve the usual geometric relationships among activations remains unresolved.",
      "Swapping activations of selected neurons and measuring the score difference between two choices does not establish that a whole layer restores the most likely token. Some supporting layer-ablation results measure only how much the next layer influences predictions.",
      "No study isolates self-repair as the cause of misleading estimates of component importance. Redundancy, suppression by later components (downstream erasure), and shared normalization pathways offer other explanations.",
      "Some supporting evidence still needs verification, including how tabular-model studies define the difference between repair and redundancy. It is also unclear whether later studies are independent of one another."
    ]
  }
